<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>DownloadProbe</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>download-probe</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="Siemens.Engineering">
      <HintPath>$(TiaOpennessDir)\Siemens.Engineering.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>true</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Borrows `openness-cli`'s Portal session handling, PLC device resolution, safety refusal and
    DownloadProvider walk through a named InternalsVisibleTo grant (OpennessCli/AssemblyInfo.cs).
    A second copy of device resolution would mean this tool writes to a device chosen by different
    rules from the one `download-plan` describes and `compile` gates.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpennessCli\OpennessCli.csproj" />

    <!--
      Ladder.Download (netstandard2.0, no Siemens reference, no network, no Portal) — THE LOAD
      MANIFEST PARSER, linked rather than reimplemented.

      *** THIS IS THE LIVE PATH THAT LIBRARY WAS BUILT FOR. *** DownloadResultAdapter's own docs say
      so: "the live path is DownloadResultAdapter, straight off the Openness object. Never route a
      live download through a log file: the log is the probe's RENDERING of the result, and anything
      the renderer drops is gone before this reader sees it." Until 2026-08-13 nothing did that — the
      deployment gateway (net8.0, a separate process that must never link Siemens.Engineering or every
      harness build joins the (Path,FileHash) approval cycle) recovered the manifest by parsing this
      tool's rendered log. The manifest is what `Loaded` keys on, so it is the single most load-bearing
      value this binary produces, and it was reaching its consumer by scraping.

      Referencing it here rather than writing a second parser keeps ONE opinion about what a load
      manifest is: this binary's JSON and the harness's reading of it are now the same code.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\download-feedback\DownloadFeedback\DownloadFeedback.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    DELIBERATELY NO `ApproveForOpenness` POST-BUILD TARGET, unlike OpennessCli.csproj.

    That target writes the TIA Openness whitelist entry a human would otherwise approve at a dialog
    (FI-74). It is the right trade for a read-mostly CLI that gets rebuilt constantly. It is the
    wrong one here: this is the only binary in the repo that can transfer a program to a controller,
    and self-approving it would mean a `dotnet build` silently grants Portal access to a
    download-capable executable nobody looked at.

    Approving this binary is therefore a deliberate act, taken when execution is authorised:
        tools\openness-approve-build.ps1 -Exe <path to download-probe.exe>
        tools\openness-approve-build.ps1 -Status        # read-only: is it approved right now?
    Until then the first attach hangs until the connect timeout expires, or throws
    EngineeringSecurityException — both mean "needs approval", not "Portal is wedged".
  -->

</Project>
